1
00:00:02,040 --> 00:00:04,080
Good morning. Good morning.

2
00:00:04,120 --> 00:00:07,240
Jason, I just got my first fan letter from my column.

3
00:00:07,400 --> 00:00:08,640
Good. That's wonderful.

4
00:00:08,680 --> 00:00:09,260
What's it say?

5
00:00:10,180 --> 00:00:14,420
Dear Miss Malone, I've never really given much thought to kitty litter, but your

6
00:00:14,420 --> 00:00:16,380
recent article opened my eyes.

7
00:00:16,680 --> 00:00:21,200
Now I swear by Tabby Fresh, perhaps one day I'll get a cat.

8
00:00:26,940 --> 00:00:29,980
Morning. Morning. Um, Dad, I'm sorry.

9
00:00:29,980 --> 00:00:33,960
Dad, I don't want you to be mad, but I can't find my history book.

10
00:00:34,340 --> 00:00:34,980
Why would I be mad?

11
00:00:35,080 --> 00:00:37,360
I'm not the one who has to go to class without a book, Ben.

12
00:00:37,700 --> 00:00:40,160
I'm not the one who has to take a test unprepared.

13
00:00:40,580 --> 00:00:43,660
No, but you're the one who has to cough up 25 bucks to replace it.

14
00:00:44,580 --> 00:00:45,820
No, no, no, no, no, no.

15
00:00:45,820 --> 00:00:46,900
I'm sorry you're not listening, Ben.

16
00:00:47,040 --> 00:00:48,740
No, I'm not gonna bail you out this time.

17
00:00:50,960 --> 00:00:51,740
You're right, Dad.

18
00:00:52,100 --> 00:00:53,720
It's time for a tough love lesson.

19
00:00:54,540 --> 00:00:55,900
I'm gonna drop out of fifth period.

20
00:00:57,460 --> 00:00:58,900
I can always sell Mary Kay.

21
00:00:58,900 --> 00:01:00,160
I've got good scanning.

22
00:01:01,760 --> 00:01:03,560
Enough, Ben. Jason, give him $25.

23
00:01:04,120 --> 00:01:06,700
Ugh. I've only got two 20s.

24
00:01:06,820 --> 00:01:07,500
You're a prince.

25
00:01:08,820 --> 00:01:10,980
Hello. Ah, scramble, please.

26
00:01:11,940 --> 00:01:15,820
Hey, Mike, did it ever occur to you that your mother is not just some domestic

27
00:01:15,820 --> 00:01:22,800
drudge? No. Uh, Dad, listen, I'm having a little problem with one of my college

28
00:01:22,800 --> 00:01:25,680
classes. Attending? No, no, Dad.

29
00:01:25,680 --> 00:01:27,740
I just can't seem to find my sociology book.

30
00:01:27,740 --> 00:01:33,660
Oh. And you're probably gonna have to sell Mary Kay if I don't come up with 25 bucks

31
00:01:33,660 --> 00:01:34,380
to replace it.

32
00:01:35,100 --> 00:01:36,800
No, it was Thigh Master's.

33
00:01:38,080 --> 00:01:41,240
But, uh, but, actually, it was $35.

34
00:01:41,900 --> 00:01:46,140
Mike, Ben just took your father's last $40 for his lost history book.

35
00:01:46,880 --> 00:01:48,820
Uh, that little leech.

36
00:01:50,900 --> 00:01:53,260
Well, I hope you can eat Mike's eggs, too.

37
00:01:53,360 --> 00:01:55,940
Sorry. Ah! Okay, I'll try.

38
00:01:57,740 --> 00:02:00,620
Debbie Tigard won the Pulitzer Prize for journalism?

39
00:02:01,260 --> 00:02:04,640
Debbie who? My old college roommate.

40
00:02:04,960 --> 00:02:06,300
Somebody you know won the Pulitzer?

41
00:02:06,740 --> 00:02:09,180
That's great. Oh, yeah.

42
00:02:09,420 --> 00:02:13,060
Just the kind of good news you want to start your day.

43
00:02:27,740 --> 00:02:39,180
We'll be nowhere near the best is ready to begin Oh, oh, oh As long as we got each

44
00:02:39,180 --> 00:02:49,080
other We got the world spinning right in our hands But you and me, we gotta be The

45
00:02:49,080 --> 00:02:57,700
luckiest dreamers who never quit dreaming As long as we keep on giving We can take

46
00:02:57,700 --> 00:03:00,180
anything that comes our way.

47
00:03:00,200 --> 00:03:03,940
Baby rain or shine, all the time.

48
00:03:04,620 --> 00:03:10,600
We got each other sharing the laughter and love.

49
00:03:12,580 --> 00:03:16,440
Sharing the laughter and love.

50
00:03:24,400 --> 00:03:27,920
A friend of yours wins the Pulitzer and you puree the paper?

51
00:03:28,280 --> 00:03:30,640
Jason, don't you remember Debbie Tygard?

52
00:03:31,060 --> 00:03:33,340
The one who made my life a living hell for four years?

53
00:03:33,460 --> 00:03:34,400
It doesn't ring a bell.

54
00:03:34,660 --> 00:03:37,200
Debbie, if he's breathing, I'll jump him.

55
00:03:37,400 --> 00:03:41,360
Tygard? Oh, yeah.

56
00:03:41,960 --> 00:03:44,320
Yeah, short, short brunette, shag haircut.

57
00:03:44,500 --> 00:03:45,440
I knew it.

58
00:03:45,440 --> 00:03:47,020
I -I always said no.

59
00:03:49,520 --> 00:03:51,800
Jason, she'd do anything to get ahead.

60
00:03:51,900 --> 00:03:53,740
Remember my old journalism professor, Mr.

61
00:03:53,880 --> 00:03:57,700
Rutger? Oh, I bet she was sleeping in a canopy bed.

62
00:03:59,040 --> 00:04:01,600
Don't get mad, but I lost my spelling book.

63
00:04:03,880 --> 00:04:08,580
Would this, by any chance, cost, oh, say, $35 to replace?

64
00:04:09,180 --> 00:04:13,600
Uh -oh. Give it up, Mike.

65
00:04:13,600 --> 00:04:15,040
I had to try.

66
00:04:17,020 --> 00:04:19,360
No, Jason, life is funny.

67
00:04:20,120 --> 00:04:25,440
Here, Debbie Tygard is picking up a Pulitzer, and I'm picking up soggy Cheerios.

68
00:04:25,860 --> 00:04:28,140
You're also overreacting, Maggie.

69
00:04:28,780 --> 00:04:32,840
You are not just a person who does menial chores all day.

70
00:04:33,400 --> 00:04:36,300
Mr. Spot. You are...

71
00:04:36,300 --> 00:04:40,660
You're a great housewife, Maggie, but you're also a damn fine journalist.

72
00:04:40,760 --> 00:04:46,600
Oh, sure. Here Debbie's writing about acid rain, and I'm writing about, well, I'll

73
00:04:46,600 --> 00:04:48,460
just say it, kitty -doo -doo.

74
00:04:49,800 --> 00:04:52,460
Yeah, but you wrote the hell out of that.

75
00:04:53,700 --> 00:04:55,520
Jason, I don't understand it.

76
00:04:55,520 --> 00:04:58,840
Debbie couldn't string two sentences together, and she wins the Pulitzer?

77
00:04:59,600 --> 00:05:01,620
She was probably sleeping with the judges.

78
00:05:02,160 --> 00:05:06,480
Oh! You think I'm being petty, don't you?

79
00:05:06,500 --> 00:05:07,580
No, no, no, no.

80
00:05:08,100 --> 00:05:09,780
You're right, I am.

81
00:05:09,780 --> 00:05:13,640
You know, maybe Debbie's turned herself into a real journalist.

82
00:05:14,180 --> 00:05:16,200
You know, I'm a big enough person.

83
00:05:16,720 --> 00:05:19,300
I'm going to call and congratulate her.

84
00:05:19,360 --> 00:05:27,600
That's better. Yes, Debbie Tygarden -Peachers, please.

85
00:05:28,240 --> 00:05:31,660
Yes, this is an old friend of hers, Maggie Malone from the Long Island Sentinel.

86
00:05:32,820 --> 00:05:39,480
Yes, seriously. She can return my call in August?

87
00:05:42,140 --> 00:05:46,780
Well, yes. Yes, I would like to, uh, leave a message.

88
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:58,760
Okay. Let's go over this again.

89
00:05:59,260 --> 00:06:01,280
You're in the park, you see the babe.

90
00:06:01,720 --> 00:06:02,300
What do you say?

91
00:06:03,040 --> 00:06:05,280
I'm Chrissy and I'm lost.

92
00:06:05,680 --> 00:06:07,200
Can you help me find my brother Ben?

93
00:06:07,640 --> 00:06:09,240
My studly brother Ben.

94
00:06:09,240 --> 00:06:09,460
I'm sorry, I'm sorry.

95
00:06:10,840 --> 00:06:12,540
And then I get the ice cream?

96
00:06:12,780 --> 00:06:15,620
Tell you what, I'll get you some ice cream, and you don't have to do anything.

97
00:06:15,820 --> 00:06:17,500
Great. I'll go get my coat.

98
00:06:18,600 --> 00:06:20,220
You find your own babes.

99
00:06:22,800 --> 00:06:24,120
Hey, what are you doing?

100
00:06:24,160 --> 00:06:25,660
You blew my scam, Sam.

101
00:06:26,040 --> 00:06:27,220
Now I'm blowing yours.

102
00:06:27,340 --> 00:06:29,140
You knew I had a whole lost book thing ready.

103
00:06:29,360 --> 00:06:31,300
So I got there first.

104
00:06:31,500 --> 00:06:36,160
Listen, Junior, I perfected that scam while you were still saying, Pasquetti.

105
00:06:37,080 --> 00:06:39,160
Can I get Rocky Road?

106
00:06:40,080 --> 00:06:44,220
Absolutely. And remember, Chrissy, who do we ask for when we need a babysitter this

107
00:06:44,220 --> 00:06:46,720
Saturday night? My studly brother Ben.

108
00:06:52,860 --> 00:06:54,060
What's all that stuff?

109
00:06:54,320 --> 00:06:56,600
Some of my old college papers.

110
00:06:57,960 --> 00:07:01,000
An award? Why isn't this on the wall?

111
00:07:01,300 --> 00:07:06,620
Oh, it's just a little award, but I did beat out students from 50 schools including

112
00:07:06,620 --> 00:07:07,580
Harvard and Yale.

113
00:07:08,960 --> 00:07:12,060
Wow. 1969? This thing's an antique.

114
00:07:14,040 --> 00:07:17,040
What's that? A list of goals I made when I was 20.

115
00:07:17,960 --> 00:07:19,860
Become the first woman war correspondent.

116
00:07:20,540 --> 00:07:24,080
Learn Swahili. Have a novel published by age 35.

117
00:07:25,320 --> 00:07:28,020
I can't believe you actually did all these things.

118
00:07:28,460 --> 00:07:29,380
Way to go, Mom.

119
00:07:29,660 --> 00:07:32,060
Oh, well, not everything, I'm afraid.

120
00:07:32,060 --> 00:07:33,180
Just the ones that are checked.

121
00:07:33,800 --> 00:07:34,660
Oh, come on.

122
00:07:34,660 --> 00:07:36,260
I mean, you did a lot of things here.

123
00:07:36,440 --> 00:07:38,960
I mean, how many people have actually, um...

124
00:07:43,300 --> 00:07:46,080
Um... Learned to drive a stick shift.

125
00:07:58,280 --> 00:08:01,420
Either I'm hugging a pillow or we've got to join a gym.

126
00:08:06,100 --> 00:08:09,040
Oh, Maggie, not that old list again.

127
00:08:09,660 --> 00:08:12,460
Can you believe I never toured the Soviet Union?

128
00:08:14,740 --> 00:08:15,920
It's good you didn't.

129
00:08:16,060 --> 00:08:17,580
Now there is no Soviet Union.

130
00:08:17,680 --> 00:08:18,500
Check it off.

131
00:08:20,840 --> 00:08:22,860
Fallen nations don't count.

132
00:08:22,940 --> 00:08:23,960
It was a goal.

133
00:08:24,540 --> 00:08:27,840
Boy, this Debbie Tygard thing is really good to you, isn't it?

134
00:08:27,840 --> 00:08:29,160
Not at all.

135
00:08:29,360 --> 00:08:34,740
I could care less the Debbie vacuum lips Tygard I lucked into a stinkin' Pulitzer.

136
00:08:36,940 --> 00:08:38,760
Well, I'm glad to see it's out of your system.

137
00:08:40,260 --> 00:08:44,080
Jason, this may have started out with Debbie, but now it's about me, my failed

138
00:08:44,080 --> 00:08:47,360
goals. I bet you accomplished everything on your list.

139
00:08:49,380 --> 00:08:53,540
Well, Peggy, come on, I've hit my share of pain and disappointment.

140
00:08:53,680 --> 00:08:56,740
What? You never got to meet Herb Albert in the Tijuana brass?

141
00:08:59,020 --> 00:09:00,460
I met the brass.

142
00:09:01,860 --> 00:09:04,080
Thank you. That makes me feel so much better.

143
00:09:04,620 --> 00:09:06,940
Oh, you've had a lot of success, Maggie.

144
00:09:07,040 --> 00:09:09,060
You worked at Newsweek and Channel 19.

145
00:09:09,220 --> 00:09:09,860
It doesn't count.

146
00:09:09,900 --> 00:09:10,700
I quit both of them.

147
00:09:10,780 --> 00:09:13,940
Yes, but you made a decision to do that because you want to spend more time with

148
00:09:13,940 --> 00:09:17,900
your family and to write that book of yours, which I know you're gonna get around to

149
00:09:17,900 --> 00:09:20,200
someday. Jason, don't you see?

150
00:09:20,300 --> 00:09:23,980
This list was made by a woman with dreams who wasn't afraid to test herself.

151
00:09:24,380 --> 00:09:26,140
Now, I know exactly when I got off track.

152
00:09:26,140 --> 00:09:27,180
It was 1972.

153
00:09:27,220 --> 00:09:28,140
Do you remember that year?

154
00:09:28,140 --> 00:09:34,020
Not specifically. I was covering the election in South Dakota, and I had the chance

155
00:09:34,020 --> 00:09:37,560
to follow George McGovern into a helicopter and get an exclusive interview.

156
00:09:37,980 --> 00:09:39,320
I don't remember that interview.

157
00:09:39,900 --> 00:09:41,580
Because I didn't get the interview.

158
00:09:42,180 --> 00:09:44,640
I was too scared to get into the chopper.

159
00:09:44,660 --> 00:09:46,660
And I've been living in the comfort zone ever since.

160
00:09:46,920 --> 00:09:50,080
Come on, Maggie, you've done all kinds of uncomfortable things.

161
00:09:50,180 --> 00:09:54,240
Such as? Such as you got tear gassed when you protested against apartheid.

162
00:09:54,280 --> 00:09:55,400
You gave birth.

163
00:09:55,400 --> 00:09:58,040
You saw Mike in Streetcar Named Desire.

164
00:10:00,400 --> 00:10:01,460
Face it, Jason.

165
00:10:01,540 --> 00:10:02,180
I'm a quitter.

166
00:10:02,820 --> 00:10:03,980
You're not, Maggie.

167
00:10:04,240 --> 00:10:05,240
You're still a young woman.

168
00:10:05,360 --> 00:10:07,300
You got all kinds of time to do whatever you want.

169
00:10:07,420 --> 00:10:09,860
Not if I put it off for one more day.

170
00:10:10,380 --> 00:10:13,320
Jason, I'm gonna master the things on this list.

171
00:10:13,340 --> 00:10:15,060
Okay. Starting with number six.

172
00:10:15,100 --> 00:10:16,980
Number six. Rappel down a mountain.

173
00:10:17,060 --> 00:10:20,860
Oh, no. I need to challenge myself.

174
00:10:21,440 --> 00:10:22,380
All right, yeah.

175
00:10:22,380 --> 00:10:23,920
Well, then try number 18.

176
00:10:23,920 --> 00:10:28,300
Learn to play the sitar, all the challenge of rock climbing, and a much higher

177
00:10:28,300 --> 00:10:33,380
survival rate. Jason, I don't want to spend the rest of my life hiding from the

178
00:10:33,380 --> 00:10:34,200
things that frighten me.

179
00:10:34,360 --> 00:10:38,280
Okay, fine, fine, but rappelling down the mountain, Maggie, come on, you can't be

180
00:10:38,280 --> 00:10:41,620
serious. Okay, you're serious, but do I have to go with you?

181
00:10:42,420 --> 00:10:46,300
Oh, now, see, this double figure eight, Maggie, this is great, I mean, you can hold

182
00:10:46,300 --> 00:10:46,980
an elephant with that.

183
00:10:47,100 --> 00:10:47,540
Look at that.

184
00:10:47,860 --> 00:10:51,120
Oh, whoa! Hold on.

185
00:10:51,360 --> 00:10:52,580
You've been practicing, haven't you?

186
00:10:52,580 --> 00:10:53,740
Oh, a little bit.

187
00:10:53,960 --> 00:10:56,640
A little. Every night this week, she's tied something up.

188
00:10:59,360 --> 00:11:00,940
Wipe that smirk off your face.

189
00:11:04,080 --> 00:11:06,520
Oh, whoa. Uh, guess today's the big day, huh?

190
00:11:06,700 --> 00:11:07,600
Yeah, you bet.

191
00:11:07,600 --> 00:11:10,560
Today we conquer a sheer rock face and see what we're made of.

192
00:11:10,920 --> 00:11:14,260
As long as what we're made of doesn't end up spread all over the sheer rock face.

193
00:11:15,540 --> 00:11:16,740
Oh, relax, Dr.

194
00:11:16,880 --> 00:11:18,820
Seaver. This is gonna be a day that you'll always remember.

195
00:11:19,420 --> 00:11:21,040
And this time, I'm sure the ropes are gonna hold.

196
00:11:24,440 --> 00:11:28,180
So, um, you guys are gonna be gone all day and won't be back till tomorrow, right?

197
00:11:28,420 --> 00:11:31,940
Well, we'll be last through to Monday if your mom decides to bag herself a bar.

198
00:11:33,940 --> 00:11:35,200
Okay. Okay, let's get the gear loaded.

199
00:11:35,960 --> 00:11:36,940
Okay. Okay, I'll see you later.

200
00:11:36,940 --> 00:11:38,760
Bye, boys. Take good care of each other.

201
00:11:39,180 --> 00:11:40,240
Have fun. Be safe.

202
00:11:40,360 --> 00:11:42,660
We will. Make sure Chrissy goes to college.

203
00:11:42,960 --> 00:11:45,380
Oh, Dan. All right, we'll miss you.

204
00:11:49,680 --> 00:11:51,140
Oh, come on, Jason.

205
00:11:52,100 --> 00:11:56,180
Keep up! Oh, so exhilarating in this fresh crisp air.

206
00:11:56,920 --> 00:11:58,540
I have a rock in my shoe.

207
00:11:59,840 --> 00:12:01,560
Oh, what a perfect day to conquer your fears.

208
00:12:01,640 --> 00:12:04,140
Take a deep breath and step off the edge of the world.

209
00:12:05,760 --> 00:12:07,020
Well, here we are.

210
00:12:09,880 --> 00:12:11,980
Well, that doesn't look as bad as I thought.

211
00:12:13,620 --> 00:12:32,180
Maggie? Maggie? Maybe you can't keep hugging this mountain all night long.

212
00:12:32,700 --> 00:12:36,200
Watch me. Jason, how could you let me talk you into this?

213
00:12:36,500 --> 00:12:38,300
I'm just here for moral support.

214
00:12:38,580 --> 00:12:40,840
Stop that! I want physical support.

215
00:12:40,920 --> 00:12:42,360
I want an elevator, a ski lift.

216
00:12:42,480 --> 00:12:43,780
I'll even get in a helicopter.

217
00:12:44,540 --> 00:12:49,380
She's panicking. Well, if worse comes to worse, I can always pry her off the rock

218
00:12:49,380 --> 00:12:50,160
with a crampon.

219
00:12:50,940 --> 00:12:53,680
Is there anything you could say to assuage your fears?

220
00:12:54,340 --> 00:12:57,340
Okay. What does assuage mean?

221
00:12:58,360 --> 00:12:59,980
You know what litigation means.

222
00:13:00,580 --> 00:13:02,000
Jason, don't blame Kent.

223
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:03,260
I thought I could do this.

224
00:13:03,460 --> 00:13:05,240
Would you like me to give her my customary pep talk?

225
00:13:05,300 --> 00:13:06,300
That would be nice.

226
00:13:06,660 --> 00:13:11,460
Okay. What goes up must come down.

227
00:13:14,580 --> 00:13:15,820
Remember that, Jason.

228
00:13:16,180 --> 00:13:17,320
I want it on my tombstone.

229
00:13:18,860 --> 00:13:21,100
Peggy, all I can tell you is what you were telling me.

230
00:13:21,520 --> 00:13:24,940
Conquering this mountain is the first step in getting everything else you want.

231
00:13:25,100 --> 00:13:26,900
Jason, how does that help me win a Pulitzer?

232
00:13:28,520 --> 00:13:29,800
Good point. You want to leave?

233
00:13:30,720 --> 00:13:32,140
Jason, you can't let me quit.

234
00:13:32,260 --> 00:13:33,500
I thought you wanted to quit.

235
00:13:33,540 --> 00:13:35,020
Well, of course I do.

236
00:13:35,960 --> 00:13:39,320
Which is exactly why I can't.

237
00:13:39,680 --> 00:13:40,440
That a girl.

238
00:13:40,440 --> 00:13:41,760
Touch me and you die!

239
00:13:43,360 --> 00:13:45,580
Okay, now this'll be just like we practiced.

240
00:13:45,780 --> 00:13:47,860
Okay. You wanna keep your knees bent.

241
00:13:49,320 --> 00:13:52,200
You wanna stay focused, okay?

242
00:13:52,320 --> 00:13:54,760
Uh -huh. Most of all, relaxed.

243
00:13:54,860 --> 00:13:56,100
Just like having a baby.

244
00:13:56,820 --> 00:13:57,760
Can I have an epidural?

245
00:13:58,880 --> 00:14:01,980
You ready? As ready as I'll ever be.

246
00:14:02,400 --> 00:14:05,400
Okay, I'm gonna lower you down the mountain slowly.

247
00:14:05,660 --> 00:14:06,100
Nice and easy.

248
00:14:06,340 --> 00:14:07,320
